# XCU ulimit
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# ulimit is intrinsic (XCU 1.7). Limits are changed only inside a subshell
# command substitution or a subshell, and only downwards, so no test
# needs privilege and the shell running the test keeps its limits. The
# -a form and the single-resource forms are Base (Issue 8 moved ulimit out
# of XSI); only the -t option stays XSI. The page's synopsis still carries
# a stray XSI marker on the single-resource form (standard issue).

assertion  XCU.ulimit.001
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       ulimit -a exits zero and writes at least six lines (one for each
           of the resources the option list names that are not shaded),
           each holding a limit value: decimal digits or unlimited.
note       The format of a line is unspecified apart from the form of the
           value, so a line is judged only by holding a word that is
           digits or unlimited.
test       t/001-all.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.002
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      2024
says       ulimit -f, -S -f and -H -f each write one line holding decimal
           digits or the word unlimited; ulimit with no option writes
           what ulimit -f writes, and with -f and no -H or -S the soft
           limit is the one reported.
test       t/002-single-value.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.003
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      2024
says       Each of -c, -d, -n, -s and -v, alone, exits zero and writes
           one line holding decimal digits or unlimited.
test       t/003-each-option.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.004
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       ulimit -S -f n lowers only the soft file-size limit: the new
           value is read back by ulimit -S -f and the hard limit is as it
           was.
test       t/004-soft-only.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.005
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      2024
says       With neither -H nor -S, a new limit is set for both the hard and
           the soft limit.
test       t/005-both.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.006
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      2024
says       A soft limit cannot be set above the hard limit: the request
           exits greater than zero and the soft limit is unchanged.
test       t/006-above-hard.sh

assertion  XCU.ulimit.007
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/ulimit.html#STDOUT
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2024
says       ulimit -t exits zero and writes one line holding decimal digits
           or unlimited.
test       t/007-cpu-time.sh
